## 1️⃣  Highest‑risk correctness bug

**Bug:** *When a session is restored from disk the server may think it is in the `"running"` phase while the gameplay threads that the scheduler needs are still missing.*  
The logs (`restore‑incident.log`) repeatedly show:

```
WARN scheduler skipped agent turn because gameplay thread missing
WARN client observed session.state phase=running while no thread ids were present
```

The scheduler (`nextSchedulableAgentId`) will never pick an agent because `this.threadIds.gameplayByAgentId` is empty, yet the server continues to emit a `session.state` with `phase: "running"` and `connected: false`.  This mismatch can cause:

* The UI to think the world is live while no agent can actually act.
* The server to dead‑lock (no turn ever starts, no metrics advance, no world tick progresses).
* Subsequent restore attempts to fail silently because the code never re‑initialises the missing threads.

### Root cause (code)

In `GameRuntimeServer.restoreSession()` the method loads a saved snapshot and **does not guarantee that the gameplay threads exist** before setting `phase = "running"`.

```ts
private async restoreSession(): Promise<void> {
  const saved = await this.stateStore.load();
  if (!saved) { return; }

  const content = this.contentStore.getContent();
  if (saved.simulation) {
    this.simulation = Simulation.fromState(saved.simulation, content);
    this.seed = saved.simulation.seed;
    for (const agent of saved.simulation.agents) {
      this.agentConfigs.set(agent.id, { id: agent.id, name: agent.name });
    }
    this.resetAgentLoopState((saved.simulation.agents ?? []).map((agent) => agent.id));
  } else if (typeof saved.seed === "number") {
    // fallback single‑agent session
    this.simulation = new Simulation(saved.seed, DEFAULT_WORLD_SIZE, DEFAULT_WORLD_SIZE, content, [
      { id: "agent-1", name: "Agent 1" }
    ]);
    this.seed = saved.seed;
    this.agentConfigs.set("agent-1", { id: "agent-1", name: "Agent 1" });
    this.resetAgentLoopState(["agent-1"]);
  }

  this.phase = "running";               // <-- set too early
  this.preparedSeed = null;
  this.threadIds = { gameplayByAgentId: {} }; // <-- empty if threads were not restored yet
  this.threadToAgentId.clear();
  this.activeTurn = null;
  this.schedulerIndex = 0;
  await this.logReplay("session.restored", {
    seed: this.seed,
    tick: this.simulation.getState().tick
  });
}
```

* `restoreSession` restores the simulation state **but never restores the Codex threads** (`threadIds.gameplayByAgentId`).  
* The `threadIds` are only populated in `startSession` (when a fresh session is created) or in `startThreadsForExistingSession` (which is called only when `this.simulation && Object.keys(this.threadIds.gameplayByAgentId).length === 0 && !this.threadIds.builder`).  
* After a restore, the code falls through to `phase = "running"` **without ever calling `startThreadsForExistingSession`**, so `threadIds.gameplayByAgentId` stays empty.  
* The scheduler (`nextSchedulableAgentId`) checks `this.threadIds.gameplayByAgentId[agent.id]` and will always skip, emitting the warning seen in the logs.

### Smallest safe fix

Add a **single, deterministic step** after the snapshot is loaded that *ensures the gameplay threads exist* before the server advertises `"running"`.

1. **Extract the thread‑bootstrap logic** that already exists in `startSession` into a reusable private method `bootstrapThreadsForAgents(agentIds: string[])`.  
2. Call that method from `restoreSession` **before** setting `phase = "running"`.  
3. Keep the existing `startThreadsForExistingSession` unchanged – it is used for fresh sessions; the new method will be a thin wrapper that re‑uses the same Codex RPC calls.

